# Explicit syntax notes belong at first use, not only in a glossary at the end.
ML[1]['explain'] += ' type(value) reports the type of a value; the word inside parentheses is its input.'
ML[4]['explain'] += ' Square brackets after a list retrieve a position; after a dictionary they retrieve a key. A colon separates each dictionary key from its value.'
ML[6]['explain'] += ' A dot accesses a named property or operation of an object: sales.shape reports row and column counts; head() shows the first rows. An operation called on an object is a method. to_csv writes a CSV; read_csv reads one. index=False tells pandas not to save its extra row labels. A word followed by = inside a call supplies a named option. Empty parentheses mean no arguments are supplied.'
ML[7]['explain'] += ' sum() adds the selected numbers; mean() averages them. The comparison produces one true/false value per row; the outer brackets keep the true rows.'
ML[8]['explain'] += ' statistics.mean and statistics.median are ready-made functions in the imported statistics library. max and min return the largest and smallest list values. Adding two lists with + joins their items; it does not add corresponding items.'
ML[9]['explain'] += ' None represents a missing entry in this example. isna() marks missing cells as True and others as False; summing those Boolean values counts the missing cells. drop_duplicates() removes identical rows; copy() makes a separate table to work on. pandas mean ignores missing entries here, so state that it describes observed prices only.'
ML[10]['explain'] += ' pyplot is the chart interface inside matplotlib; plt is its short name. bar takes categories and heights. ylabel and title add labels; ylim(bottom=0) sets the bottom of the value axis; show displays the figure.'
ML[12]['explain'] += ' sum adds list values and len counts them. abs gives a number\'s distance from zero. One quarter is 1/4 = 0.25 = 25%; a proportion becomes a percentage when multiplied by 100.'
ML[13]['explain'] += ' A class such as LinearRegression is a model recipe; calling it with parentheses creates a model object. A nested list has one inner list per row. from a_library import a_name loads the named tool.'
ML[14]['explain'] += ' Four names on the left receive the four outputs of the split in order: training inputs, test inputs, training targets, test targets. DummyRegressor is a baseline model; strategy="mean" predicts the training-target mean. Chaining fit after the constructor creates and trains that model. random_state is for repeatability, not a quality score.'
ML[15]['explain'] += ' load_iris() returns a dataset object. data.data holds the input table; data.target holds the category codes. stratify=data.target preserves approximate class proportions when dividing records. max_depth=2 permits at most two question levels; random_state fixes randomness for repeatability. accuracy_score compares actual labels with predicted labels.'
ML[16]['explain'] += ' labels=[0,1] fixes the displayed row and column order in this two-class example. The final test must stay separate from any choices made after examining validation errors.'

# Both beginner tracks teach Python before pandas. SQL lessons retain separate .sql files.
DA = [{k: (v.replace('ML', 'DA') if isinstance(v,str) else v) for k,v in item.items()} for item in ML[:11]] + DA
assert len(ML)==len(DA)==24

DA[8]['explain'] += ' A proportion is a part divided by a whole; multiply by 100 to express a percentage. For 12 successes in 20 attempts, 12 / 20 * 100 is 60%. Always name the denominator before comparing rates.'
DA[8]['demo'] += '\nprint(12 / 20 * 100)'
DA[8]['expected'] += ' The percentage is 60.0.'
DA[8]['task'] += ' Compare 12 successes out of 20 with 15 out of 50. Calculate each percentage and explain why the larger count need not mean the better rate.'
DA[8]['answer'] += '\n# Rates are 60% and 30%; denominators differ.'

